<title>USA: S.Africa sugar may top 2.5 mln tonnes - US attache.</title>
<headline>S.Africa sugar may top 2.5 mln tonnes - US attache.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-04-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Plentiful rainfall could boost South Africa's 1998 sugar crop to about 2.5 million tonnes, allowing exports to top 1 million tonnes again, according to a U.S. attache report made public on Thursday.</p>
<p>Sugar production rose to 2.4 million tonnes during the 1997 milling season, up from 1.77 million tonnes during 1996, said the report dated April 9.</p>
<p>&quot;Rainfall for the new season has also been very good and an even better crop of about 2.5 million tons is forecast for 1998,&quot; the report said. &quot;This will allow exports in excess of one million tons again.&quot;</p>
<p>U.S. attache reports are not official U.S. Agriculture Department data.</p>
